
# COM3505 Lab Assessment 1 #

## Our Project ##
We decided to work with the Lilygo T-Watch-2020. We wanted to try and make a few apps for it that each showcased parts of the watch's possibile functionality. The main aspects we wanted to show off were the touch screen, accelerometer, vibrator and bluetooth capabilities. The apps we decided on were:
* A date/time display with a corresponding app to edit the time.
* An app which allows the user to enable/disable the device's bluetooth.
* A snake game which uses the accelerometer to dictate the snake's movement direction. The user can also connect to the watch via bluetooth and see a live feed of what is happening in the game. When the snake crashes into a wall or itself, the watch will vibrate.

## File Structure ##
* The `.pio` file is located in the `/LA2/ProjectThing` directory, which is where this project should be run. It contains the following files:
* `ProjectThing.ino` - This file contains all of the functionality for our project.
* `config.h` - This file contains the header files we included for our project to function. The important inclusions are:
    * `LilyGoWatch.h` - This is part of the TTGo Watch library found on github [HERE](https://github.com/Xinyuan-LilyGO/TTGO_TWatch_Library)
    * `BluetoothSerial.h` - This is from the BluetoothSerial library which allowed us to form a bluetooth connection with other devices.
    * `soc/rtc.h` - This is included in the ESP32 Arduino core, and it allowed us access to low-level real-time clock functionalities.

## Running the Project ##
### Setting Things Up ###
This section covers the steps that should be followed before uploading anything to the arduino. We will cover how to set up if you are using either Visual Studio Code (VSCode) or Arduino IDE.
#### Visual Studio ####
1. Ensure you have the `PlatformIO IDE` and `ESP-IDF` extensions in VSCode. If you do not, they will need to be installed.
![VSCode extensions](Images/VSCodeExtensions.png)
2. You now need to clone this repository to your desired location and open the `LA2` folder in VSCode. Assuming you have followed step 1, VSCode should now set up the `.pio` and `.vscode` folders.
3. In the PlatformIO taskbar at the bottom of VSCode, make sure that the project environment is `env:TTGo_TWatch`, and the upload port is correct for you (for us it was COM8).
![VSCode board selection](Images/VSCodeBoardSelection.png)
4. You should now be ready to build and compile by clicking the upload button.

#### Arduino IDE ####
1. Open the boards manager in the Arduino IDE and ensure that you have `esp32` provided by Espressif, keep in mind the version must be 2.0.16 or earlier or it might encounter issues with a newer version. 

2. Install the following libraries:
    * BluetoothSerial by Henry Abrahamsen through Library Manager in your Arduino.
    * TTGO Watch, to use the TTGO Watch library, follow these steps:
        1. Download the ZIP file of the library from GitHub: [TTGO TWatch Library](https://github.com/Xinyuan-LilyGO/TTGO_TWatch_Library).
        2. Open the Arduino IDE.
        3. Go to the top menu and select **Sketch**.
        4. Click on **Include Library**.
        5. Select **Add .ZIP Library**.
        6. Locate and select the downloaded ZIP file to add the library to the Arduino IDE.

3. Ensure the correct board and port are selected (see images): 
* In the board selection, choose TTGO T-Watch.
* For the port, select the appropriate COM port corresponding to your device
![Arduino IDE board selection](Images/ArduinoIDEBoardSelect.png)
4. You should now be ready to build and compile by clicking the upload button.

### Using the project ###
So you have now set up, built and uploaded the project to your watch. 
* You should be greeted with a screen that displays the current time and date.

![Watch time display](Images/TimeDisplay.png)
* If you touch anywhere on the screen, it will take you to a menu with three options. If you select the middle option, it will open the corresponding app, otherwise it will move the selected outer option into the middle so that you can now open it. 

![Watch menu](Images/Menu.png)
* Set Time - This app will display the current time with four functional buttons. 'H+' and 'H-' increase/decrease the hour accordingly and the same goes for 'M+' and 'M-' for minutes. If you click the 'back' button, you will go back to the time display you were initially greeted with.

![Watch set time app](Images/SetTime.png)
* BlueTooth - This app will display a slider to enable/disable the watch's BlueTooth functionality. If it has a red square on the left, it is disabled while a blue square on the right means it is enabled. Again, there is a 'Back' button which does the same as in the 'Set Time' app.

![Watch BlueTooth app](Images/BlueTooth.png)
* Snake - This app will display a black screen with a green snake and a red apple. You will instantly start to move forward and you can change the movement direction by tilting your watch in that direction. You will feel the watch vibrate slightly whenever the snake's direction changes and a longer vibration when the snake crashes into a wall/itself to indicate the end of the game. Once the game has ended, you will be presented with two options 'Again' and 'Back' which have self explanatory functions.

![Watch snake app](Images/Snake.png)
* Snake + BlueTooth - If you have enabled BlueTooth, you can also connect another device and use a BlueTooth serial monitor to get a textual live feed of what is going on in the game. For our demonstration we used the android app [Serial Blutooth Terminal](https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=de.kai_morich.serial_bluetooth_terminal&hl=en&gl=US&pli=1)

![Other device BlueTooth serial monitor](Images/SerialBlueToothTerminal.png)


